      "page": 4,
      "text": "4. Requirements\n\n4.1. General Requirements\n\nReproductions must closely resemble the appearance and proportions of the sources.\n\nAny metal part of the helmet must conform to the “Armors and Weapons Aesthetics and Decorations”\ndocument.\n\nAny textile or leather part of the helmet must conform to the “Textiles and Leather” document.\n\n4.2. Visor\n\nThe visor must not be too long or too short, it must respect the proportions of historical sources.\nBreath holes should be round and consistently sized unless documented by an historical source.\n\nBreath holes must not be so numerous that they compromise the helm’s structural integrity. It is\nacceptable to mirror breath holes to both sides.\n\n4.3. Eye slots\n\nEye slots should resemble sources for the visor.\nEye slots should not exceed 25mm tall to prevent any injury.\nExternally riveted Eye bars must be based on documented historical examples.\n\nGreat helms with two (2) Eye slots are permitted to add up to two (2) externally riveted Eye bars or\nsafety Eye bars per Eye slot.\n\nGreat helms with a single Eye slot are allowed to add up to five (5) safety Eye bars: one (1) in the middle\nand two (2) on each side.\n\nIt is not possible to mix externally riveted Eye bars and safety Eye bars on the same helmet. Only one\ntype of Eye bar is allowed. Integrated Eye bars are not allowed.\n\nEye bars must conform to the “Eye bars” document.\n\nGreot Helms v. 261 4/7",
